# Animals of savanna, desert, badlands and jungle.
# These biomes (and mangrove swamp) are "tropical" in this pack. Serene Seasons gives them a
# dry and a wet half-year, not four seasons. So the rows here have TWO
# numbers:   group = dry wet
#
# Savanna animals are tied to water and rain, not to temperature. The
# real pattern is the same on every continent. In the wet season water
# is everywhere and herds spread out. In the dry season they crowd
# around the few places that still have water. Seen from one patch of
# savanna away from the water, the dry season is the thin one.
#
# Every big savanna animal follows the same logic, so their numbers sit
# close together: 1.0 in the wet half, 0.5 to 0.9 in the dry half, with the
# strongest rain-followers (zebra) at the bottom and the animals that
# range freely over the whole region (giraffe) at the top. Animals tied
# to water drop most. Predators follow their prey.

[elephant]
animals    = naturalist:elephant
confidence = sourced
source     = https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2018.00167/full
why        = Elephants have to drink every two to three days. In the dry season GPS
             tracking shows them making longer, straighter trips toward water and
             gathering at the few waterholes that last. In the wet season they spread
             across the whole range. A patch of savanna away from water holds fewer in
             the dry half. The drop is small because they do not leave their range.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.8  1.0

[zebra]
animals    = naturalist:zebra
confidence = sourced
source     = https://www.cms.int/fr/node/41605
why        = Plains zebra travel with the wildebeest in the Serengeti and Mara. Rainfall
             drives the loop. Herds leave the dry plains for the Mara River region and
             come back with the rains. The wet-season range is where they calve. So a
             patch of dry-season savanna is mostly empty of them. They are the strongest
             case of rain-following in the pack, so they get the biggest dry cut.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.5  1.0

[hippo]
animals    = naturalist:hippo
confidence = sourced
source     = https://news.ucsb.edu/2019/019687/hippos-hidden-world
why        = In the dry season a river shrinks to scattered pools and the hippos pack into
             them. Large young males travel up to 15 km upstream to find water. In the
             wet season water is plentiful and they spread out and move downstream. Away
             from the last pools, the dry half is thin. The source is one river system
             in Tanzania and it mostly tracked males.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.5  1.0

[ostrich]
animals    = naturalist:ostrich
confidence = sourced
source     = https://agris.fao.org/search/zh/records/65df76284c5aef494fe2c9bb
why        = Ostriches time their breeding to the rains so that chicks hatch when food
             is greener. Birds in drier country wander to follow rain. A 15-year study in
             the Mara and Serengeti found a main breeding peak in February and a minor one
             in October. Sources disagree on the exact months, so this only models "more
             in the wet half, fewer in the dry half". The size of the drop is a guess.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.7  1.0

[vulture]
animals    = naturalist:vulture
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://hawkmountain.org/blog/in-the-field/thermal-scouting
why        = Vultures soar on thermals, which are strongest in hot, dry weather, and they
             feed on carcasses. Dry-season die-offs of grazers feed them. The wet half
             has more food of other kinds and weaker lift, so they are somewhat fewer
             in the sky. This is reasoned. The page above only covers thermals. It does
             not say anything about dry-season die-offs. The size is a guess.
#                 dry  wet
savanna  = 1.0  0.7
desert   = 1.0  0.7
badlands = 1.0  0.7

[komodo-dragon]
animals    = naturalist:komodo_dragon
confidence = sourced
source     = https://journal.ugm.ac.id/jtbb/article/download/48280/27394
             https://flore.unifi.it/retrieve/eab0d6df-ed42-4e0e-ab7d-b282c1c9bea7/Jessop_et_al_2022.pdf
             https://zoobarcelona.cat/en/node/231
why        = Komodo dragons live on a few dry, volcanic islands in eastern Indonesia, in
             tropical savanna and open dry forest. Most of the rain falls from December to
             March. A 2022 tracking study found they move more in the dry season. The
             dry half is also mating and nesting time. They are not a rainforest or
             desert animal, so desert is cut and jungle is cut to a small chance.
             Naturalist spawns them in all four. Sparse jungle is open enough that the
             small chance stays.
#                 dry  wet
desert   = 0.0  0.0
jungle   = 0.3  0.3
savanna  = 1.0  0.7
badlands = 1.0  0.7

[capybara]
animals    = ambient_creatures:capybara
confidence = reasoned
why        = Capybaras live beside water in tropical South America. Where there is a
             dry season they gather at the last wet places. A patch of savanna away
             from water holds fewer of them in the dry half. This is well-known
             but no source was read for it. Swamp and jungle are wet all year here, so
             they get no rule.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.5  1.0

[tortoise]
animals    = naturalist:tortoise
confidence = reasoned
why        = Two different lives share one animal here. In desert, tortoises spend most
             of their time in burrows. They come out to feed after rain and shelter
             through the hottest, driest weather, so the dry half is thin. In swamp,
             where there is a real cold season, they go dormant in winter like other
             reptiles (see snakes in reptiles.rules for the sourced version of that
             behaviour). Nothing here is sourced. The one news page found about a Nevada
             tortoise leaving its burrow in spring gives no timings.
#                dry  wet
desert = 0.6  1.0
#                 spring summer autumn winter
swamp  = 0.7  1.0  0.8  0.1

[rhino]
animals    = naturalist:rhino
confidence = sourced
source     = https://rhinoresourcecenter.com/wp-content/uploads/2019/12/1577270031.pdf
             https://ielc.libguides.com/sdzg/factsheets/whiterhino/behavior
why        = Black rhinos must drink every 24 to 48 hours and stay loyal to a permanent
             water source. A Kruger study found they have smaller home ranges and stick
             closer to one place in the dry season, which points to surface water as the
             limit. White rhinos' home ranges shrink in the dry season too, and they
             travel daily to the few permanent waters. So a patch of savanna away from
             water holds fewer rhinos in the dry half. They stay in the region, so the
             cut is modest.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.8  1.0

[lion]
animals    = naturalist:lion
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4929767
why        = Lions follow their prey. Studies in Etosha and the Serengeti found they hunt
             near ambush cover in the wet season and near permanent water in the dry
             season, where prey is easy to predict. The herbivores in these files crowd
             water in the dry half, so lions do too. A patch of savanna away from
             water holds fewer of them then. This is a milder cut than the zebra's
             because a pride covers a lot of ground. I did not read that paper end to
             end, so the link is background and the numbers are reasoned.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.85 1.0

[giraffe]
animals    = naturalist:giraffe
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://publish.csiro.au/WR/fulltext/WR13211
why        = Giraffe browse trees and do not depend on grass or standing water the way
             zebra and rhino do. They breed all year, and birth peaks follow local rain
             but differ between sites (wet season in Namibia, dry season in Tarangire,
             August to September near Nairobi). So there is no clear direction at the
             population level. The one small effect is that in the dry months they
             gather where the leaves and water last. That makes the cut mild. The source
             is the rainfall and birth-timing paper, which shows the sites disagree. It
             does not show the gathering, which is reasoned.
#                dry  wet
savanna = 0.9  1.0

[armadillo]
animals    = minecraft:armadillo
confidence = reasoned
source     = https://gadnrle.org/sites/default/files/Armadillo%20Fact%20Sheet_UGA.pdf
why        = Nine-banded armadillos eat insects and worms dug out of soil. Cold is what
             limits their range, and they do not hibernate. Cold does not exist in the
             tropical biomes here, so the only seasonal lever is moisture: dry ground
             is harder to dig and holds fewer worms and grubs. That part is reasoned. The
             fact sheet covers cold and burrows, not rainfall. Savanna and badlands are
             where the game spawns them.
#                 dry  wet
savanna  = 0.7  1.0
badlands = 0.7  1.0

[camel]
animals    = minecraft:camel
confidence = sourced
source     = https://animaldiversity.org/accounts/Camelus_dromedarius
             https://www.cms.int/sites/default/files/publication/WildCamel_GreatGobi_MNG.pdf
             https://www.publish.csiro.au/RJ/RJ09050
why        = Camels live in desert with a long dry season and a short rainy one. They
             are nomads. They move toward rain and long distances to oases for water and
             plants. In Australia, feral camels moved into areas that got more rain in
             the warm months, though the sample was small. In the dry half they crowd the
             water, so a stretch of open desert is emptier. Camels are rare in the game
             already (weight 1), so this makes them rarer.
#               dry  wet
desert = 0.7  1.0

[desert-scorpion]
animals    = naturalist:desert_scorpion
confidence = reasoned
why        = Scorpions are night hunters that shelter in burrows by day. Desert
             invertebrates tend to come out and feed more when the ground is damp, so
             the dry half is quieter above ground. This is general desert ecology. No
             source was read for it, and the size of the cut is a guess. The jungle
             scorpion lives in a wet, warm place all year and gets no rule.
#                 dry  wet
desert   = 0.8  1.0
badlands = 0.8  1.0
